# The working-tree pass of `mise run scan-secrets` reads this file: it is .trufflehog-exclude plus the CHANGELOG.md entry at the end. Keep the shared lines identical (a spec enforces it).
# Regex patterns, one per line, matched against repository-relative paths.
# .kore/ holds local key material; no scanner may ever read it.
^\.kore/
(^|/)node_modules/
(^|/)dist/
(^|/)bin/
(^|/)coverage/
# SOPS-encrypted files are expected ciphertext (ENC[...] values), not leaked secrets.
\.enc\.yaml$
# Vendored upstream Keycloak CRD: the JDBC detector reports a documentation example sentence in an upstream description (no host, no credential). The current file is trimmed and scans clean; the line stays only while the branch history holds the original, and can go once the change is squashed.
(^|/)infra/charts/keycloak-operator/templates/crd-keycloaks\.yaml$
# release-please's generated CHANGELOG.md links every entry to its 40-hex commit hash, and scanning the whole file reports one such hash as an unverified GitHubOauth2 token (release PR #735, line 634). It is a commit link, not a credential. Only this pass skips the file: the history pass still reads every committed version of it, so a real token there is caught.
^CHANGELOG\.md$
# The browser token check's fixture: a fake session token, assembled from its segments in the file, that the token check's tests assert against. It is not a credential. The path stays excluded only while the branch history holds the earlier literal form (commit 69ea3632), and can go once the change is squashed.
^tools/kore/src/testing/signin-token-program\.fixtures\.ts$
